===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# {{alternative spelling of|en|naive}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Henry Rutgers Marshall|title=The True, the Good and the Beautiful|editors=[[w:James Edwin Creighton|J[ames] E[dwin] Creighton]]; w:James Seth|journal=w:The Philosophical Review|volume=XXXI|issue=5 (whole 185)|location=New York, N.Y.; Lancaster, Pa.|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longmans, Green, & Co.]]|month=September|year=1922|section=section II|page=453|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/philosophicalrev31cornuoft/page/453/mode/1up|passage=But it is this '''naïve''' man—and philosophers for the greater part of their lives are '''naïve''' men—who makes the unrecognized introspective observations with which we are here concerned. It is the '''naïve''' man who divides the Real into the Beautiful, the True and the Good.}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} {{feminine of|en|naïf|cap=1}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Paul Morand|title=Paris Letter|editor=w:Scofield Thayer|journal=w:The Dial|volume=LXXVII|location=Camden, N.J.|publisher=The Dial Publishing Company, Inc.|month=July|year=1924|month_published=September|year_published=1924|page=241|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_dial_1924-09_77/page/241/mode/1up|passage=He has his own definite qualities of composition: a '''naïf''' grace, a deft handling of forms, and the introduction of variations into landscapes, figures, or the eyes of American sailors. {{...}} In this room, blue as a tunnel with stagnant smoke, '''naïve''' girls dance with the directors of musical reviews.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ernest Boyd|title=Readers and Writers|journal=[[w:The Independent|The Independent: A Weekly Journal of Free Opinion]]|volume=120|issue=4058|location=New York, N.Y.|date=10 March 1928|page=235|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_independent_1928-03-10_120_4058/page/235/mode/1up|columns=2–3|passage=The passages are alike because (1) the person who is supposed to be writing is illiterate; and because (2) she or he is '''naïf''' and engagingly childish. {{...}} Gertrude Stein may be falsely '''naïve''', but what are two realistic satirists of American types, like Anita Loos and Ring Lardner, doing in such a category?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard Cody|chapter=Fiduciary|chapter_number=III|title=Perfect Witness: Being a Sequel to a Tale of 1951 Entitled ‘Neighbouring Eyes’|location=[Bloomington, Ind.]|publisher=w:Xlibris|year=2009|page=46|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/perfectwitness0000rich/page/46/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-4415-8239-3|passage=Don’t be '''naïve''', Valija. You’re letting sexual feelings think for you, as usual. {{...}} His worst pretence is a faith in good manners. This may make him, in some respects, a snob. But if any one of us is '''naïf''', it is not I; it is he—and in the best possible way.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Brother Tom [''i.e.'', Thomas Paonessa]|chapter=Essence|title=The Angel and the Rowboat|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=w:AuthorHouse|year=2014|section=part II (The Problem)|page=129|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5o4_8sYX4mQC&pg=PA129|isbn=978-1-4184-4730-4|passage=His faux '''naïf''' had her completely fooled. {{...}} Profoundly '''naïve''', she had absolutely no experience of pain or suffering or death and saw nothing in the odd reptilian-skinned stranger but the opportunity to make a new friend.}}

====Usage notes====
* ''Naive''/''naïve'' is most often treated as gender-neutral. Usage of both ''naïf'' and ''naïve'', the latter specifically for women, following French, is rare.
* See {{section link|''naive''#Usage notes}} for the use of the diaeresis.

